## Solution

The statement is **false** in general. I provide a counterexample.

### Counterexample

Let $S = \omega_1$ (the first uncountable ordinal), and let

$$\mathcal{C} = [S]^{\leq\omega} = \{A \subseteq S : A \text{ is countable}\}.$$

**$\mathcal{C}$ is nonempty.** The empty set is countable, so $\emptyset \in \mathcal{C}$.

**$\mathcal{C}$ has no maximal element with respect to inclusion.** Let $A \in \mathcal{C}$. Then $A$ is countable, while $S = \omega_1$ is uncountable, so $A \neq S$ and there exists $\alpha \in S \setminus A$. The set $A \cup \{\alpha\}$ is still countable, hence belongs to $\mathcal{C}$, and strictly contains $A$. Thus no element of $\mathcal{C}$ is maximal.

**Every countable chain in $\mathcal{C}$ has an upper bound in $\mathcal{C}$.** Let $(A_n)_{n \in \omega}$ be a countable chain in $\mathcal{C}$, i.e. $A_0 \subseteq A_1 \subseteq A_2 \subseteq \cdots$, with each $A_n$ countable. The union

$$U = \bigcup_{n \in \omega} A_n$$

is a countable union of countable sets, hence countable. Therefore $U \in \mathcal{C}$. Moreover $A_n \subseteq U$ for every $n$, so $U$ is an upper bound of the chain in $\mathcal{C}$.

Since every countable chain in $\mathcal{C}$ admits an upper bound in $\mathcal{C}$, there is **no** countable subcollection of $\mathcal{C}$ that is a chain with no upper bound.

### Conclusion

The collection $\mathcal{C} = [\omega_1]^{\leq\omega}$ is nonempty, has no maximal element under inclusion, yet every countable chain in it has an upper bound. This contradicts the claimed statement, so the statement as written is **false**.

The true result behind this is Zorn's lemma (contrapositive): a poset with no maximal element must contain *some* chain with no upper bound — but that chain need not be **countable**, as the counterexample above shows (the witnessing chain would have to be uncountable, e.g. a cofinal chain in $[\omega_1]^{\leq\omega}$ of length $\omega_1$).

### PROOF COMPLETE
